    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d353">

                <pb facs="0413" n="329" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg329"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 851.711/277</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Sharp</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-02-19T18:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 19, 1918, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 6.11 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3217. Your 3103, January 23, and 3183, [February] 14.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d353-f1"/> Am informed by Foreign Office that as a matter of
                    principle the French censorship permits to pass: (1) remittances for payment on
                    patents or more generally for the maintenance of their validity; (2) documents
                    having object of obtaining for Allies new patents in enemy countries when
                    examination has ascertained that no injury would result thereby to national
                    defense. Foreign Office’s note follows by next pouch.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sharp</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
